Okay, I know it's been a little longer this round, but here we go...

C343-148 - Unknown, but I did discover that upon waking up
           the display is not properly updated until you move.
C343-149 - Info available.
C343-150 - The following do not have a gender specified
           (in monst.c):  The Grand Master, The Arch Priest,
           The Minion of Huhetotl, Ixoth, Master Kaen, Nalzok,
           Scorpius, The Master Aassassin, and the Dark One.
           I'm guessing some of those should have a fixed gender.
           As for the size, the encyclopedia entries for Orion
           and for the Norn seem to imply they should be larger
           (currently they are MZ_HUMAN, i.e., medium size).
C343-151 - Info available.
C343-152 - Well, you can tell whether it's lit or not.
C343-153 - Confirmed.  When a guardian naga spits venom at the
           player, it blinds.  When a player polyselfed into a
           guardian naga spits venom at a monster, it burns.
C343-154 - Unsure.  Falling into lava does destroy them, but
           zapping with a wand of fire doesn't seem to, nor
           falling into a fire trap.  Tried both repeatedly.
           Obviously, if you light them on purpose they will
           burn down over time.
C343-155 - Some of the capitalized entries in bogusmons should
           get the article ("Ravenous Bugblatter Beast of Traal",
           "Klingon"), and others should not ("Smokey the bear",
           "King Kong").  How can the code tell which is which?
           I propose that bogusmons needs to indicate somehow.
           See also C343-9, and it is possible that something
           similar to that might happen in other circumstances.
           Confirmed:  "The Audrey II", "The Totoro", "The Barney
           the Dinosaur", "The Morgoth", "The Godzilla", "The
           Smokey the bear".
C343-156 - An identify scroll (even a formally identified one)
           always says "This is an identify scroll".  This would
           frequently be redundant even if the identify scroll
           were itself formerly unidentified.  Similarly, a
           scroll of genocide always says, "You have found a
           scroll of genocide!"  This is redundant, since it's
           about to ask you what kind of monster to genocide.
C343-157 - Worms *shouldn't* be described as having scales, but
           they have M1_SLITHY, which causes mbodypart to return
           snake_parts[part], which in some cases says "scale".
           Since M1_SLITHY has other implications as well, I
           imagine the proper fix is to add worm_parts[] and
           teach mbodypart when to use it preferentially.
C343-158 - This is similar to C343-157:  in norse mythology
           krakens are regarded as having tentacles, but
           mbodypart doesn't seem to know about that.
C343-159 - Similar to C343-156 and 157: mbodypart uses vortex_parts
           for all v and E, so their head is called a "central
           core", of all things.  Stalkers should probably get
           humanoid_parts instead of vortex_parts.
